af(2,'bm21-workforce-planning','2.1','sl','Workforce planning','human resource planning','Forecasting the number and skills of employees required / and planning how the organization will obtain, develop and retain those people.','Explain two factors affecting human resource planning at {name}.',[
('Mali Care','Mali provides home care. Its district’s population is ageing, and public transport no longer runs late enough for evening visits.', ['An ageing population can increase demand for care.','Mali may need more carers and specialist skills in supporting older adults.','Failing to plan for that demand can leave evening visits unfilled.'],['Reduced transport limits labour mobility.','Potential carers without cars cannot reach late visits.','Mali may need transport support or different schedules to secure an adequate evening workforce.']),
('Nali Games','Nali develops games. A new project needs animation skills absent from its team, while experienced animators increasingly prefer remote freelance work.', ['A new project changes the required skill mix.','Current programmers cannot automatically supply specialist animation.','Nali must plan training or additional skills before the project reaches its animation stage.'],['Changing work preferences affect labour supply.','Remote freelance work may be more attractive to experienced animators.','Nali may need flexible arrangements to access skills rather than assume local full-time recruitment is feasible.']),
('Ona Foods','Ona processes vegetables. A change in seasonal migration reduces available harvest workers, while new sorting equipment reduces manual inspection tasks.', ['Migration changes the external labour supply.','Fewer seasonal workers can create a harvesting bottleneck.','Ona may need earlier staffing arrangements to avoid vegetables remaining unprocessed.'],['Technology changes internal skill requirements.','Sorting equipment reduces manual tasks but needs trained operators.','Ona should plan redeployment and technical training instead of simply keeping the old staffing mix.']),
('Pali Travel','Pali runs city tours. Weekend demand is rising, but many guides seek flexible hours because of family commitments. A new district requires another language.', ['Employee availability affects scheduling.','Flexible-hour preferences can limit weekend staffing.','Pali must match tours to agreed availability or it may advertise trips it cannot staff.'],['Market expansion changes required skills.','The new district needs guides with an additional language.','Existing headcount alone cannot show whether Pali has the capability to deliver those tours.'])])
ef(2,'bm22-resistance-change','2.1','sl','Managing resistance to change','resistance to change','Employee opposition or reluctance toward a proposed change / arising from concerns such as uncertainty, loss of security, status or established routines.','introducing the proposed change with staff participation',[
('Qali Logistics','Qali proposes route-planning software. Drivers fear it will intensify workloads, although managers predict fewer empty journeys. The launch date is six weeks away.','Participation can reveal operational knowledge;drivers can identify unrealistic delivery times before Qali configures the software','Consultation takes time and may raise expectations;Qali may miss its launch if managers cannot resolve every preferred route','Use a time-limited pilot with driver representatives, training and explicit workload safeguards; retain a final decision deadline.','No pilot measures the effect on actual driving time or breaks.'),
('Rali Museums','Rali wants staff to sell memberships instead of only checking tickets. Employees value cultural education and fear being judged solely on sales.','Participation can align roles with employee purpose;staff can propose membership conversations that support Rali’s educational mission','Employees may resist targets even after consultation;membership income may not rise quickly enough to meet the funding gap','Agree mixed service and membership measures and train staff; revise targets if sales pressure undermines visitor education.','Visitor interest in paid membership has not been measured.'),
('Sali Furniture','Sali plans flexible teams replacing specialist departments. Skilled carpenters fear losing status; managers expect faster order completion. Current delivery delays are serious.','Employee involvement can reduce status fears;carpenters can help define skilled responsibilities within the new teams','A long design process may worsen delays;customers already waiting for furniture may cancel orders','Pilot one team with clear skill recognition and delivery measures before restructuring all departments.','The sources of current delivery delays have not been separated.'),
('Tali Publishing','Tali proposes an online editing platform. Editors fear loss of autonomy and exposure of unfinished work. Remote collaboration currently produces duplicate versions.','Participation can improve acceptance;editors can shape permissions and review stages to protect unfinished drafts','Consensus may delay necessary standardization;Tali may continue costly version errors while preferences are debated','Introduce a small pilot with clear draft visibility, training and a dated review; standardize once major concerns are tested.','The platform’s effect on error rates and editing speed is unverified.')],exam='paper-1')

# Read an actual reporting structure rather than infer a span from headcount alone.
for name,roles,teams in [('Uli Motors',['Production','Sales','Finance'],[4,3,2]),('Vali Events',['Venues','Bookings','Catering'],[3,5,4]),('Wali Repairs',['Repairs','Parts','Accounts'],[6,2,2]),('Xali Farms',['Growing','Packing','Sales'],[5,4,3])]:
    body='<rect x="290" y="10" width="180" height="50" fill="#e0f2fe" stroke="#172554"/>'+svg_text(315,40,'Managing director')
    for j,(role,staff) in enumerate(zip(roles,teams)):
        x=25+j*250
        body+=f'<path d="M380 60 L380 85 L{x+105} 85 L{x+105} 110" fill="none" stroke="#172554"/><rect x="{x}" y="110" width="210" height="50" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#172554"/>'+svg_text(x+12,140,role+' manager',14)+f'<path d="M{x+105} 160 L{x+105} 200" fill="none" stroke="#172554"/><rect x="{x}" y="200" width="210" height="50" fill="#e0f2fe" stroke="#172554"/>'+svg_text(x+15,231,f'{staff} staff report directly',14)
    items=[explain('a','State the managing director’s span of control and the number of hierarchical levels shown.',['Span of control is 3: the three functional managers report directly to the director.','There are 3 levels: director, functional managers, staff.']),explain('b',f'Explain one advantage and one disadvantage for {name} of this functional structure.',['Specialization can improve efficiency.','Grouping staff under functional managers develops expertise in the named activities.','Functional separation may create communication barriers.','An order needing several departments could be delayed if managers pursue separate priorities.']),explain('c','Explain one effect of removing the functional-manager level while all staff report to the director.',['The director’s span of control would widen.',f'It increases from 3 managers to {sum(teams)} staff, so there are more direct relationships to supervise.','Communication can become more direct but the director may lack time for specialist support.','This may speed simple decisions while weakening coordination if the director cannot handle the wider span.'])]
    add(2,'bm23-org-chart','2.2','sl',f'{name}: reading a functional chart','Organizational structure',f'{name} uses the reporting structure shown. Each staff member reports only to the functional manager immediately above them. Managers report only to the managing director. There are no omitted reporting levels.',items,exam='paper-1',svg=svgwrap(body,780,280),check=f'Director has3 direct reports; levels3; after delayering direct reports={sum(teams)}. Headcount not confused with direct reporting span.')
af(2,'bm24-delegation','2.2','sl','Delegation','delegation','Passing authority to a subordinate to carry out a task / while the manager retains ultimate accountability for the result.','Explain two effects of delegating the proposed authority at {name}.',[
('Yali Hotels','Yali lets reception staff refund up to USD 80 for minor service failures. Previously all refunds needed the general manager, who was often away.', ['Customer problems can be resolved faster.','Reception no longer waits for the absent manager to approve small refunds.','This can restore trust before a disappointed guest leaves Yali.'],['Employees need guidance and training.','Inconsistent refunds could appear unfair or encourage unnecessary compensation.','A clear USD 80 limit and records can protect Yali while staff learn the authority.']),
('Zali Labs','Zali delegates routine consumables purchasing to team leaders, capped at USD 500 weekly. Scientists previously waited for a director to sign every order.', ['Routine work can proceed with fewer delays.','Leaders can replenish consumables without director signatures.','Zali’s experiments may avoid downtime from missing low-value supplies.'],['Delegation can increase spending risk.','Separate teams may duplicate purchases or choose inconsistent products.','Shared records and the weekly cap help Zali keep authority within its budget.']),
('Ami Studios','Ami allows project designers to agree minor client revisions without the creative director. The director remains responsible for project quality.', ['Designers gain responsibility and autonomy.','They can act on minor feedback without waiting for the director.','This may improve motivation and speed Ami’s revision process.'],['Quality may become less consistent.','Designers could interpret the permitted scope differently.','Ami needs clear limits and review points so delegated revisions preserve its design standards.']),
('Bami Retail','Bami delegates local display choices to store supervisors. Headquarters still determines safety standards and the overall brand identity.', ['Displays can respond to local knowledge.','Supervisors know which products their customers request.','Bami may sell stock more effectively than with one identical national display.'],['Coordination requires clear boundaries.','Local choices could conflict with brand presentation or safety rules.','Headquarters must specify non-delegated standards while allowing useful display flexibility.'])])
ef(2,'bm25-decentralization','2.2','sl','Decentralization','decentralization','Dispersing decision-making authority away from the top of an organization / so lower levels or local units make a greater share of decisions.','decentralizing the proposed decisions',[
('Cami Clinics','Cami’s head office approves all opening hours. Rural clinics want hours matching local bus arrivals, but finance staff worry that different schedules will raise staffing costs.','Local decisions can match patient access;bus-based opening hours may reduce missed rural appointments','Coordination and cost control become harder;different schedules may require additional cover and inconsistent staffing','Delegate opening hours within service and staffing-budget limits, then compare access gains with cover costs.','Clinic-level patient and staffing data have not been supplied.'),
('Dami Foods','Dami manages shops in four regions. Local managers want to choose some products because diets differ, while headquarters negotiates discounts from large uniform orders.','Local assortment may improve demand matching;regional diets can be served more closely','Purchasing economies may be lost;smaller orders of different products could weaken Dami’s supplier discounts','Allow a defined local range while keeping common staple purchasing centralized; review waste and margins by region.','The size of supplier discounts lost under local ordering is unknown.'),
('Eami Software','Eami’s product teams want authority to set release dates. Customers dislike delays caused by central approval, but all products use the same security team.','Teams can respond faster to customer needs;release dates need not wait for routine central approval','Shared capacity may be overloaded;several teams could book security reviews for the same date','Delegate dates subject to shared security-capacity coordination and minimum testing gates.','The security team’s capacity and current delay causes are unmeasured.'),
('Fami Schools','Fami operates private schools with different language needs. Principals want local curriculum choices; the board markets a consistent educational standard.','Local authority improves relevance;principals can choose content reflecting pupils’ language requirements','Quality may become uneven;parents may receive different learning experiences under the same Fami brand','Set common outcomes and assessment while delegating local content; audit results rather than requiring identical lessons.','There is no evidence that the proposed local choices preserve common outcomes.')],exam='paper-1')
ef(2,'bm26-leadership','2.3','sl','Leadership styles','situational leadership','Adapting the leadership approach to the circumstances / such as the urgency of a task, employee competence and the nature of the decision.','using the proposed leadership approach',[
('Gami Theatre','Gami’s director proposes democratic planning for a new season. Experienced performers want input, but an opening-night electrical fault requires an immediate safety decision.','Participation may strengthen creative commitment;experienced performers can contribute ideas to the season','Consultation is unsuitable for every decision;an immediate electrical safety issue cannot wait for a group vote','Use democratic planning for creative choices and directive action during the urgent safety problem; match style to the decision.','The time available for planning and staff experience beyond performers are unknown.'),
('Hami Bakery','Hami’s founder proposes autocratic control after repeated food-safety failures. Experienced bakers say they can suggest improvements, while new weekend staff lack training.','Clear instructions can reduce unsafe variation;inexperienced weekend staff need consistent hygiene rules','Excluding experienced staff may reduce motivation and useful ideas;bakers with process knowledge could disengage','Use directive safety rules immediately, then involve experienced bakers in improvement once compliance is secure.','The causes of failures and previous training quality are not documented.'),
('Iami Design','Iami proposes laissez-faire leadership for a team of expert designers. Two recent recruits are unfamiliar with client deadlines, and past projects have exceeded budgets.','Autonomy can support creativity;experienced designers can choose methods suited to specialist work','Limited guidance may allow coordination failure;new recruits and weak budget discipline could worsen overruns','Give experts autonomy within explicit deadlines and budgets while supporting new recruits more closely.','The share of overruns attributable to leadership rather than changing client scope is unknown.'),
('Jami Care','Jami’s owner proposes a paternalistic style, choosing staff schedules personally based on what she believes suits them. Several employees request direct involvement in shift choices.','Concern for welfare may build loyalty;the owner attempts to match shifts to employees’ personal needs','Assumed preferences may be inaccurate;staff asking for involvement may see imposed schedules as controlling','Combine welfare support with genuine schedule consultation; retain managerial authority for minimum service cover.','Actual employee preferences and the feasibility of requested shifts are not known.')],exam='paper-1')
ef(2,'bm27-taylor','2.4','sl','Taylor and financial rewards','piece-rate pay','A payment method based on the quantity of output produced / with a stated amount paid for each unit rather than only for time worked.','introducing piece-rate pay',[
('Kami Assembly','Kami pays assemblers hourly. Managers propose piece rates to raise output. Faults are difficult to detect until customers use the product, and workers do not control component deliveries.','A financial link to output can motivate effort;Taylor’s approach predicts more assemblies when each completed unit increases pay','Output incentives can undermine quality and fairness;workers may rush undetectable faults or lose pay when components arrive late','Pilot a quality-adjusted scheme with minimum income protection and reliable inputs; retain hourly pay if quality cannot be measured fairly.','The causes of slow output and costs of later product faults are unknown.'),
('Lami Harvest','Lami proposes paying fruit pickers by crate. Fruit quality varies between fields, and bruising reduces selling prices. Current wages are hourly.','Piece rates can reward higher productive effort;pickers receive a direct financial return for additional crates','Workers may rush or perceive unfairness;bruised fruit lowers revenue and poor fields limit crates regardless of effort','Use inspected quality standards and adjust for field conditions before trialling piece rates; a pure quantity scheme is risky.','No data show the relationship between picking speed and bruising.'),
('Mami Transcripts','Mami proposes pay per completed transcript instead of salary. Some recordings are far harder to hear, and clients penalize inaccurate names.','Output-linked pay may increase completion rates;staff gain financially from producing more transcripts','Task difficulty and accuracy complicate incentives;staff may avoid hard recordings or skip checking names','Use complexity-weighted rates and accuracy checks if reliable measurement is possible; retain salary where tasks cannot be compared fairly.','The distribution of recording difficulty and client error costs is unavailable.'),
('Nami Packing','Nami proposes piece rates for packing fragile glassware. Workers handle different box sizes, and damage is recorded only after delivery.','Direct monetary incentives can increase packing speed;workers see an immediate reward per finished box','Speed may increase hidden damage;post-delivery breakages are not visible in Nami’s immediate output count','Trial a quality-adjusted team scheme with delayed damage monitoring rather than an unrestricted box count incentive.','The baseline damage rate by box size and the claim delay are unknown.')],exam='paper-1')
af(2,'bm28-maslow','2.4','sl','Maslow and employee needs','motivation','The internal and external influences that stimulate action / and influence the direction, intensity and persistence of work behaviour.','Using Maslow’s hierarchy, explain two reasons the proposal might not motivate all employees at {name}.',[
('Oami Finance','Oami offers prestigious project awards. Several temporary employees worry about contract renewal, while experienced permanent analysts ask for more challenging work.', ['Temporary employees may prioritize safety needs.','An award does not resolve uncertainty about continued income.','Oami’s prestigious recognition may have limited effect while renewal remains unclear.'],['Experienced analysts may seek self-actualization.','Recognition alone does not provide challenging development.','Oami must offer meaningful complex work if the analysts’ main unmet need is growth.']),
('Pami Meals','Pami offers social outings after work. Some employees struggle to afford daily meals, while night workers cannot attend the outings.', ['Basic physiological needs may be unmet.','Social events do not solve difficulty paying for food.','Pami may need adequate dependable pay before outings become a strong incentive.'],['Belonging opportunities are uneven.','Night workers cannot attend the scheduled activity.','The same outing may strengthen daytime ties while making night staff feel excluded.']),
('Qami Crafts','Qami increases pay for all staff. Skilled artisans say they want recognition and freedom to design, while recent recruits need stable income.', ['Esteem needs may matter to skilled artisans.','A general pay rise does not specifically recognize craftsmanship.','Qami may need visible recognition rather than assume money addresses every need.'],['Self-actualization may depend on creative work.','A pay rise does not give artisans freedom to develop designs.','The proposal may motivate recruits seeking income more than skilled staff seeking creative growth.']),
('Rami Parks','Rami offers training for management roles. Seasonal staff have insecure hours, and several permanent staff prefer expert gardening work rather than management.', ['Safety needs may dominate for seasonal staff.','Management training does not guarantee regular hours or income.','Rami should not assume progression is attractive before basic employment security.'],['Development needs differ among individuals.','Expert gardeners may seek mastery rather than a management title.','A specialist training pathway may meet their growth needs better than the proposed management course.'])])
ef(2,'bm29-herzberg','2.4','sl','Herzberg and job design','job enrichment','Redesigning work to add meaningful responsibility, autonomy or challenge / rather than merely increasing the number of similar tasks.','introducing the proposed job-enrichment scheme',[
('Sami Service','Sami’s technicians repeat simple checks. The proposal lets them diagnose faults and own client cases. Pay errors and broken safety equipment have also caused complaints.','Responsibility can act as a motivator;owning a complete case may give technicians achievement and meaningful challenge','Unresolved hygiene problems sustain dissatisfaction;pay errors and unsafe equipment can undermine the scheme even if work is richer','Correct pay and safety failures first or in parallel, then train technicians for diagnosis and case ownership.','Technicians’ diagnostic skills and training capacity are unknown.'),
('Tami Shops','Tami wants cashiers to manage a small product category and propose displays. Staff complain that breaks are unpredictable and the store manager rejects most suggestions.','A complete product responsibility can create achievement;cashiers can see the effect of their display choices','Responsibility may be nominal;if suggestions are routinely rejected and breaks remain poor, staff may view the change as extra work','Provide real decision boundaries and reliable breaks before piloting category ownership.','There is no evidence that managers will delegate meaningful authority.'),
('Uami Records','Uami proposes giving clerks end-to-end responsibility for client files. Clerks currently enter fragments of records, and the office has crowded noisy workspaces.','Complete tasks can increase perceived significance;clerks can see how accurate files help the client','Working conditions remain a hygiene issue;noise and crowding can prevent concentration despite greater responsibility','Improve essential workspace conditions and train a pilot team for full-file ownership; check workload and accuracy.','The available space budget and current error causes are not given.'),
('Vami Makers','Vami wants operators to lead improvement projects as well as operate machines. Operators have no protected project time and fear being blamed for failed trials.','Achievement and responsibility can motivate;leading improvements gives operators influence over their own work','Poor implementation may remove the benefit;unprotected time and blame can turn enrichment into stress and unpaid extra duties','Allocate project time, support and clear learning goals before launching; do not merely add duties to unchanged output targets.','The time needed for projects and managers’ response to trial failure are unknown.')],exam='paper-1')
af(2,'bm30-training','2.4','sl','Training choices','induction training','An introduction provided when an employee joins an organization / to help the employee understand the role, procedures, colleagues and organizational expectations.','Explain one advantage and one disadvantage of the proposed training method for {name}.',[
('Wami Optics','Wami proposes on-the-job instruction for new lens-grinding operators, using its best operator as trainer while that operator still has a full production target.', ['On-the-job training uses actual equipment and tasks.','New staff learn Wami’s specific lens process rather than an unrelated example.','Skills may transfer immediately to production.'],['The trainer’s output may fall or instruction may be rushed.','A full production target competes with explaining safe techniques.','Wami could lose current output or allow faulty training to increase rejected lenses.']),
('Xami Travel','Xami proposes off-the-job language training for guides. The course gives structured practice but occurs during the busiest booking week.', ['Specialist instruction may improve language skills.','Guides can practise away from immediate customer pressure.','Better communication can improve the experience of Xami’s foreign visitors.'],['Training creates an opportunity cost.','Guides absent in the busiest week cannot lead booked tours.','Xami may need temporary cover or lose revenue unless the timing is changed.']),
('Yami Coffee','Yami will use induction sessions for new baristas before opening a branch. The sessions cover safety and service routines but do not include practice with the actual machine.', ['Induction can establish common expectations.','New baristas learn Yami’s safety and service routines before serving customers.','This can reduce avoidable uncertainty during the branch opening.'],['The induction is incomplete for practical competence.','Hearing procedures does not demonstrate safe use of the actual machine.','Yami still needs supervised practice or service errors may persist at opening.']),
('Zami Coding','Zami proposes online off-the-job security training. It uses current specialist examples, but its small team must complete it in evenings after demanding project work.', ['Specialist training can provide current knowledge.','Security examples may address risks beyond the team’s existing experience.','Zami could improve code review and reduce avoidable vulnerabilities.'],['Timing may reduce learning and motivation.','Evening study after heavy work creates fatigue.','Completion certificates may not translate into effective security practice without protected learning time.'])])
ef(2,'bm31-communication','2.6','sl','Communication channels','formal communication','Communication through officially recognized organizational channels / such as authorized reports, meetings or messages following defined responsibilities.','adopting the proposed communication method',[
('Aro Builders','Aro proposes a shared messaging app for site changes. Instructions now pass verbally through supervisors. Some contractors have unreliable mobile coverage, and safety changes must be acknowledged.','Written shared messages can reduce distortion;all connected supervisors can refer to the same site instruction','Coverage and acknowledgement remain barriers;contractors may miss safety changes despite a message being sent','Use the app with mandatory acknowledgement and a fallback briefing for offline contractors; retain a controlled safety record.','Mobile reliability and contractor access to suitable phones are unverified.'),
('Bro Books','Bro proposes weekly video meetings for remote editors instead of long email chains. Editors work across four time zones and need a searchable record of final decisions.','Video allows immediate clarification;editors can resolve ambiguous revisions rather than exchange repeated emails','Synchronous meetings may exclude staff;four time zones make attendance difficult and spoken decisions are harder to retrieve','Combine rotating video discussions with concise written decisions and accessible recordings where appropriate.','The extent of email delay and staff availability are not quantified.'),
('Cro Care','Cro proposes replacing shift-handover meetings with an electronic checklist. Carers currently share nuanced information about clients during the handover.','A checklist can standardize critical information;required entries reduce omissions across Cro’s shifts','Rich or sensitive details may be lost;a fixed checklist may not capture subtle changes in a client’s condition','Use structured electronic records plus brief discussion for unusual cases; do not treat a ticked box as proof of understanding.','No evidence shows whether handover errors come from missing records or insufficient discussion.'),
('Dro Fashion','Dro wants an internal social network for design ideas. Employees fear public criticism, while managers hope informal collaboration will reduce silos.','Open sharing can connect departments;designers may receive useful production feedback before finalizing ideas','Social barriers may suppress participation;fear of criticism could leave only confident staff contributing','Set respectful feedback rules and allow small-group trials; assess whose ideas are heard before scaling the network.','The causes of existing departmental silos have not been examined.')],exam='paper-1')

ef(2,'bm32-shamrock','2.2','ahl','Shamrock organization','shamrock organization','A flexible structure combining a core workforce with external contractors and a flexible labour group / allocating work differently according to strategic importance and demand.','moving to a shamrock organization',[
('Ero Events','Ero would retain six planners, contract catering and hire temporary hosts for peak weekends. Clients expect one consistent service standard, and the same temporary staff are not always available.','Flexibility can align labour cost with demand;temporary hosts reduce the need to pay a large permanent team during quiet weeks','Service control may weaken;changing hosts and external caterers may deliver inconsistent experiences under Ero’s name','Retain core control of client relationships and safety, qualify contractors and maintain a trained pool before reducing permanent roles.','The reliability and full coordination costs of contractors are untested.'),
('Fro Games','Fro plans a core design team, freelance animation and temporary testers. Its unreleased game designs are commercially sensitive and freelancers also work for rivals.','Specialist contracting provides skills when needed;Fro can access animation expertise without permanent employment throughout development','Knowledge and coordination risk increase;freelancers may expose sensitive ideas or be unavailable when revisions are needed','Use carefully selected contractors with clear confidentiality and integration processes; keep strategic design knowledge in the core.','Contract enforceability and freelancer availability at launch are unknown.'),
('Gro Farms','Gro proposes permanent agronomists, contracted transport and seasonal harvest staff. A recent force-field exercise records flexibility as a driving force and quality concerns as a restraining force.','Flexible employment can match seasonality;Gro avoids paying a full harvest workforce outside picking season','Quality may suffer if seasonal workers lack skills;poor picking can damage crops and weaken customer confidence','Support the change by reducing the restraining quality concern through early training and supervision rather than merely adding pressure for flexibility.','No evidence links seasonal staffing to the current damage rate.'),
('Hro Support','Hro proposes a core technical team and outsourced routine customer support. Customers often begin with simple questions that later reveal complex faults.','Outsourcing may free core experts for complex work;routine enquiries need not consume scarce technical time','Handover failures may damage service;outsourced staff may misclassify faults and make customers repeat information','Pilot clear escalation rules and a shared record before full outsourcing; retain a core service channel for complex cases.','The share of enquiries that require escalation and vendor response quality are unknown.')])
af(2,'bm33-scientific-intuitive','2.3','ahl','Scientific and intuitive management','scientific management thinking','Making management decisions through systematic observation and analysis of evidence / rather than relying only on personal instinct or experience.','Explain one benefit and one limitation of the proposed evidence-based decision at {name}.',[
('Iro Retail','Iro will choose opening hours solely from six weeks of transaction data. The owner believes a new nearby cinema will soon change evening demand.', ['Systematic evidence reduces reliance on unsupported guesses.','Transactions reveal when Iro’s existing customers actually purchase.','Staffing hours can be aligned with demonstrated demand.'],['Past data may omit future changes.','The six-week sample predates the cinema opening.','A purely historical decision could close Iro just as new evening customers arrive.']),
('Jro Hotels','Jro uses measured cleaning times to set room targets. Its most experienced supervisor warns that older rooms need more maintenance, which the averages conceal.', ['Measured times support consistent planning.','Jro can estimate staffing needs from observed work rather than arbitrary targets.','This may improve scheduling and comparisons across shifts.'],['Averages can conceal task differences.','Older rooms need maintenance not captured by one target.','Ignoring the supervisor’s contextual knowledge may create unrealistic workloads and poorer cleaning.']),
('Kro Music','Kro will select performers from streaming statistics. Its artistic director believes an emerging local style has little online data but strong live appeal.', ['Quantitative data can inform demand estimates.','Streaming figures provide comparable evidence of existing audience interest.','Kro may reduce the risk of booking performers with no established following.'],['Measurable popularity may not capture live potential.','The emerging local style has little recorded evidence.','Ignoring informed artistic judgment could prevent Kro from discovering a distinctive successful act.']),
('Lro Meals','Lro tests two delivery routes using recorded travel times. Drivers say the trial occurred during school holidays and does not reflect term-time congestion.', ['A route trial provides observable evidence.','Recorded travel times permit a direct comparison under trial conditions.','Lro can identify a potentially faster route rather than rely only on anecdotes.'],['The evidence may not be representative.','School-holiday travel differs from term-time traffic.','Lro should combine the measurements with driver knowledge and another trial before fixing schedules.'])],exam='paper-2')
for name,start,end,leavers,cost in [('Mro Labs',72,88,12,3600),('Nro Hotels',140,160,45,1800),('Oro Games',48,52,8,6200),('Pro Foods',220,180,50,1400)]:
    avg=(start+end)/2;rate=leavers/avg*100
    items=[calc('a','Calculate annual labour turnover, using average employment. Give the percentage to two decimal places.',f'Average employment=({start}+{end})/2={avg:g}; turnover={leavers}/{avg:g} × 100.',f'{rate:.2f}%.'),calc('b','Calculate the direct replacement cost if every leaver is replaced once.',f'{leavers} × USD {cost:,}.',f'USD {leavers*cost:,.2f}.'),explain('c','Explain two limitations of concluding that the turnover rate proves employees are poorly motivated.',['Turnover records departures rather than motives.','Some leavers may retire or relocate despite being satisfied.','An exit survey would be needed before attributing all departures to motivation.','An annual average can conceal timing or departmental differences.','A short restructuring or one problematic department may account for many departures.','The whole-workforce percentage alone cannot locate the problem or identify the appropriate intervention.'])]
    add(2,'bm34-labour-turnover','2.4','ahl',f'{name}: labour turnover','Labour turnover',f'{name} employed {start} people on the first day and {end} on the last day of the year. {leavers} employees left during the year. Estimate average employment as the arithmetic mean of these two counts. Direct recruitment and induction cost is USD {cost:,} per replacement. Assume every leaver is replaced once; exclude lost output and other indirect costs. Show workings.',items,kind='data-response',check=f'Average={avg}; leavers/average*100={rate:.6f}%; direct replacement={leavers*cost}. Recruitment and labour turnover are explicitly HL-only 2.4.')
af(2,'bm35-appraisal','2.4','ahl','Appraisal methods','360-degree feedback','An appraisal approach collecting feedback from several groups around an employee / such as supervisors, peers, subordinates and, where appropriate, customers.','Explain one advantage and one limitation for {name} of introducing 360-degree feedback for development.',[
('Qro Banking', 'Qro wants branch managers reviewed by staff, peers and customers. Managers fear anonymous criticism; the current appraisal only counts sales and misses coaching quality.', ['Several perspectives can reveal broader performance.', 'staff can comment on coaching that sales totals do not measure.', 'Development can therefore target coaching weaknesses that a sales-only review overlooks.'], ['Feedback may be biased or mistrusted.', 'anonymous criticism could reduce acceptance if managers cannot understand the evidence.', 'Mistrust may make managers reject useful developmental advice.']),
('Rro Films', 'Rro proposes feedback from producers, technicians and directors after each project. Teams are small and respondents may be identifiable even without names.', ['Multiple roles can improve learning.', 'technicians may reveal coordination issues invisible to the director alone.', 'The combined evidence can identify cross-role coordination skills to develop.'], ['Confidentiality is difficult in small teams.', 'fear of retaliation may make comments overly positive.', 'Fearful respondents may withhold the very weaknesses the appraisal is meant to identify.']),
('Sro Clinics', 'Sro proposes patient and colleague feedback for clinic supervisors. Patients can assess courtesy but may not judge technical decisions, and difficult cases generate more complaints.', ['Broader evidence can improve service development.', 'patient views add information about communication and access.', 'Supervisors can use this evidence to improve explanations and patient access.'], ['Respondents may judge outside their expertise.', 'complex cases can produce poor ratings unrelated to supervisory competence.', 'A misleading low score may direct training toward the wrong problem.']),
('Tro Systems', 'Tro wants remote team leaders appraised by colleagues and team members. Some leaders work with very different project teams each quarter.', ['Multi-source feedback can capture remote leadership.', 'team members observe support that a distant senior manager may miss.', 'Repeated observations can identify support skills that remote leaders need to develop.'], ['Comparability may be weak.', 'different projects and respondents can produce ratings that do not reflect actual performance differences.', 'Comparing raw ratings may incorrectly label a leader as weaker because of the project assigned.'])],exam='paper-2',scope='Current guide: this content is AO2; two applied explanation chains, no AO3 evaluation demanded.')
ef(2,'bm36-recruitment','2.4','ahl','Internal and external recruitment','internal recruitment','Filling a vacancy using an existing employee / through promotion, transfer or another internal appointment process.','filling the vacancy through internal recruitment',[
('Uro Packaging','Uro needs a production manager for new automated equipment. Its best supervisor knows the workforce but lacks automation experience; external applicants have the technical experience but not local relationships.','Internal knowledge can speed social integration;the supervisor already understands Uro’s employees and routines','The skill gap may limit performance;new automation needs expertise the supervisor does not yet have','Promote only with credible technical training or specialist support; recruit externally if the automation timetable leaves no time to develop competence.','The training duration and external candidates’ leadership ability are unknown.'),
('Vro Museums','Vro needs a digital marketing leader. Long-serving staff expect promotion, but all have worked mainly with print campaigns. Visitor numbers are falling among younger people.','Internal progression may motivate staff;visible promotion rewards long service and knowledge of Vro’s collections','A narrow candidate pool may perpetuate old methods;print experience may not address declining young audiences','Use an open internal-and-external process with digital competency evidence; appoint internally if capability can be demonstrated.','No work samples establish current employees’ digital potential.'),
('Wro Shops','Wro must replace a store manager in two weeks. A deputy has acted successfully in the role, but promoting her would leave another vacancy during the holiday peak.','The deputy offers a rapid transition;proven acting experience reduces uncertainty about leading the store','Internal promotion creates a vacancy chain;the deputy’s duties still require cover during the peak','Promote with a temporary cover plan if performance evidence is strong; compare the total vacancy cost with external recruitment.','The availability and cost of deputy cover are not provided.'),
('Xro Research','Xro seeks a team leader after conflict in a research group. Two internal candidates belong to opposing informal groups; an external candidate has led interdisciplinary teams.','Internal candidates know the research;less time may be needed to understand Xro’s projects','An appointment may deepen divisions;choosing one faction’s candidate could reduce cooperation','Use transparent criteria and consider the external candidate if neutrality and conflict-resolution skills outweigh the integration delay.','The cause of the conflict and each candidate’s mediation skills are not established.')])
ef(2,'bm37-self-determination','2.4','ahl','Deci and Ryan','self-determination theory','A motivation theory emphasizing autonomy, competence and relatedness / as psychological needs that can support sustained intrinsic motivation.','adopting the proposed autonomy-based approach',[
('Yro Design','Yro lets designers choose projects and methods. Junior designers feel isolated and ask for feedback, while senior staff welcome freedom. Client deadlines remain fixed.','Autonomy may strengthen intrinsic motivation;senior designers can choose meaningful methods rather than follow unnecessary instructions','Autonomy alone does not satisfy all needs;juniors lacking competence feedback and relatedness may feel abandoned','Retain method choice with mentoring, shared check-ins and clear deadlines; do not equate autonomy with no support.','The effect of current feedback arrangements on junior competence is unmeasured.'),
('Zro Farms','Zro gives field teams discretion over work sequence. New seasonal staff have little knowledge of crop handling, and experienced workers want more say in improvement decisions.','Employee choice can build ownership;experienced workers can organize tasks using local field knowledge','Insufficient competence may make freedom ineffective;untrained seasonal staff can damage crops despite greater discretion','Combine supervised skills training for new staff with greater decision scope for competent teams.','There is no assessment of which workers can perform tasks safely without supervision.'),
('Aru Coding','Aru offers remote staff freedom over hours. Employees like flexibility but report loneliness and difficulty obtaining technical help from colleagues.','Flexible hours can satisfy autonomy;staff can arrange work around personal constraints','Relatedness and competence support may weaken;isolated employees cannot easily obtain feedback or team connection','Keep flexibility with overlapping support hours and optional collaborative sessions; monitor whether help becomes accessible.','The required overlap and employees’ preferred contact methods are unknown.'),
('Bru Care','Bru asks carers to design personalized visits. They value making a difference but receive only ratings based on visit length and no support for difficult cases.','Meaningful choice can increase intrinsic motivation;carers can tailor work to users’ actual needs','Conflicting measurement can undermine autonomy and competence;time-only ratings discourage appropriate personalized care','Align measures with care quality and provide case support before expanding discretion; preserve safe limits and service commitments.','The reliability of quality measures and the time needed for complex visits are unknown.')])
af(2,'bm38-hl-motivation-diagnosis','2.4','ahl','HL motivation theories','expectancy theory','A theory that motivation depends on believing effort can lead to performance / performance will lead to a reward, and that reward is valued.','Explain two reasons why the stated reward may fail to motivate the employees of {name}, using relevant HL motivation theory.',[
('Cru Sales','Cru gives the same bonus to all sales staff. Experienced staff handle harder territories and believe their effort-to-reward ratio is worse than that of newer staff.', ['Equity theory focuses on perceived fairness of input–outcome comparisons.','Harder territories may require more effort for the same bonus.','Experienced staff may reduce effort if they view Cru’s reward ratio as inequitable.'],['Review bonuses or targets to reflect territory difficulty.','Cru can compare the extra effort required in experienced staff’s harder territories before setting rewards.','A transparent adjustment can improve perceived input–outcome fairness and restore effort.']),
('Dru Logistics','Dru offers a large on-time bonus. Drivers say the target is impossible because warehouse loading is late, and they do not trust that bonuses will actually be paid.', ['Expectancy is weak if effort cannot produce the target.','Late warehouse loading is outside drivers’ control.','Extra driving effort may not improve on-time performance enough to earn Dru’s bonus.'],['Instrumentality is weak if performance does not reliably produce a reward.','Drivers distrust whether Dru will pay the promised bonus.','Even drivers able to meet the target may see no reason to increase effort.']),
('Eru Labs','Eru offers promotion into management to all high-performing scientists. Some scientists prefer difficult research projects; others value close team relationships rather than authority.', ['McClelland’s need for achievement can make challenging research attractive.','A management promotion may remove time from research.','Scientists motivated by achievement may value a difficult project more than Eru’s managerial title.'],['McClelland’s need for affiliation can prioritize relationships.','Promotion may separate a scientist from the existing research team.','The reward may be unattractive to employees whose strongest need is affiliation rather than power.']),
('Fru Rooms','Fru rewards reception staff with discounted stays at its distant resort. Staff believe the performance target is achievable and the award is reliably paid, but few can afford travel.', ['Expectancy theory requires reward valence.','A discounted stay has little value if staff cannot afford transport.','Fru’s achievable target and reliable payment do not ensure motivation when the reward is unwanted.'],['Offer a choice of rewards within the same affordable budget.','Fru’s reception staff could select a locally usable benefit rather than a distant stay they cannot travel to.','A reward employees value raises valence while leaving the achievable target and reliable payment unchanged.'])],exam='paper-2')
ef(2,'bm39-culture','2.5','ahl','Organizational culture and Hofstede','organizational culture','The shared values, beliefs and norms of an organization / that influence how members behave and make decisions.','introducing one uniform culture after the proposed merger',[
('Gru Mediatech','Gru’s founders decide informally and value experimentation. It is merging with a larger firm with formal approval rules and low tolerance of mistakes. A Hofstede-based briefing warns against assuming all staff share national stereotypes.','Shared norms can improve coordination;clear common expectations may reduce conflicting project decisions after Gru’s merger','Imposition can destroy useful strengths;strict rules may suppress Gru’s experimentation and make valued staff leave','Agree essential safety and financial standards while preserving controlled experimentation; use employee evidence rather than national stereotypes.','The briefing does not measure individual employees’ preferences or actual merger synergies.'),
('Hru Hotels','Hru merges a family chain with an international operator. Family staff expect direct owner access; the operator expects formal managerial authority. A cultural-dimensions workshop suggests different attitudes toward power distance.','Common expectations may clarify authority;staff can know who makes service and staffing decisions','A single imposed hierarchy can alienate staff;loss of owner access may be interpreted as disrespect by the family chain','Create explicit escalation routes and consult teams before changing authority; use power-distance ideas as hypotheses to test.','No staff consultation establishes whether the assumed cultural difference is widespread.'),
('Iru Robotics','Iru merges a team rewarding individual patents with a team rewarding collective improvement. Managers describe this as an individualism–collectivism tension but have not surveyed employees.','Shared reward norms may reduce internal inconsistency;employees can understand which contributions the merged Iru values','Uniform rewards may demotivate one group;individual inventors and team problem-solvers may interpret fairness differently','Use mixed recognition aligned with interdependent project needs and review feedback before imposing a single reward model.','The claimed cultural pattern may reflect current incentives rather than stable preferences.'),
('Jru Foods','Jru combines a fast experimental product team with a manufacturer using detailed procedures. Managers associate the difference with uncertainty avoidance and propose eliminating all experimentation.','Common procedures can protect consistent food quality;manufacturing needs clear controls when products reach consumers','Eliminating experimentation can reduce innovation;Jru may lose new products needed for long-term demand','Separate safe test environments from controlled production; use evidence on task risks rather than general cultural labels.','No data compare experiment-related risks with the value of successful new products.')])
ef(2,'bm40-industrial-relations','2.7','ahl','Industrial relations and conflict resolution','collective bargaining','Negotiation between an employer and employee representatives / to agree employment terms or resolve workplace issues collectively.','using the proposed negotiated settlement',[
('Kru Transport','Kru faces a threatened strike over unpredictable rosters. Management proposes joint roster design with union representatives; workers seek guaranteed rest periods and customers need reliable services.','Participation can address the underlying cause;joint design gives workers influence over rest and predictability','Agreement may restrict operating flexibility;guaranteed rest can require extra cover during unpredictable demand','Negotiate minimum rest and notice periods with agreed emergency cover; cost the settlement against disruption and safety risks.','The availability and cost of relief drivers are not known.'),
('Lru Steel','Lru’s employees are working to rule over overtime pay. Both sides agree to conciliation but management wants the conciliator’s suggested settlement to be automatically binding.','Conciliation can restore communication;an independent facilitator may help Lru and employee representatives identify a voluntary agreement','The proposed expectation confuses conciliation with binding arbitration;assuming automatic enforcement could undermine trust','Use conciliation for voluntary agreement, or explicitly agree arbitration if both sides accept a binding decision.','Neither side’s minimum acceptable settlement or cash constraint is supplied.'),
('Mru Ports','Mru proposes arbitration after repeated failed pay negotiations. Both sides would accept a binding decision, but managers fear the award could exceed the current labour budget.','Arbitration may end prolonged uncertainty;a binding decision can restore port operations when bargaining has stalled','Control of the outcome is surrendered;Mru may need to fund a pay award above its preferred budget','Arbitrate if the cost of ongoing disruption outweighs the risk of an unfavorable award; plan contingencies for the result.','The probable award and cost of continued disruption are uncertain.'),
('Nru Hotels','Nru proposes a single-union and no-strike agreement in return for regular bargaining and independent dispute resolution. Several staff fear losing their strongest bargaining tool.','Predictable dispute procedures can reduce service disruption;Nru can protect bookings while providing regular employee representation','Employees surrender some industrial leverage;a no-strike agreement may weaken pressure if management delays addressing concerns','Proceed only with credible timelines, independent resolution and meaningful bargaining rights; a one-sided restriction may intensify distrust.','The independence and enforcement of the proposed dispute mechanism are unspecified.')])
